00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:50.800
So to get started, where we
are with our partnership.

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:53.860
Hortonworks and Google Cloud
accelerates Next gen Big Data

00:02:53.860 --> 00:02:54.607
in the Cloud.

00:02:54.607 --> 00:02:57.190
All right, so obviously that's
a line that came to me directly

00:02:57.190 --> 00:02:58.820
from the marketing folks.

00:02:58.820 --> 00:03:00.850
But what does this
actually mean?

00:03:00.850 --> 00:03:06.310
What this means is more options
for hybrid Hadoop deployments,

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:08.350
as well as extending
your enterprise

00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:10.960
applications into the cloud.

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:14.080
So the way this is
actually done is the GCS--

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:16.450
the Google Cloud Storage--
connector for Hadoop

00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:19.750
is now integrated into
the Hortonworks platform,

00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:21.430
and it's fully supported.

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:26.470
Hortonworks data HDP as
well as Hortonworks data HDF

00:03:26.470 --> 00:03:29.200
are both fully compatible with
the Google Cloud platform,

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:32.002
integrated, and supported.

00:03:32.002 --> 00:03:34.210
So what does this actually
mean to you as a customer?

00:03:34.210 --> 00:03:35.960
What features can you expect?

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:39.400
Well, you can definitely
expect more agility and ability

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:42.430
to run a femoral HDP workload.

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:46.750
You should also be expecting to
maintain those same enterprise

00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:48.970
applications and extend
them into the cloud.

00:03:48.970 --> 00:03:52.270
So more specifically, things
like your metadata management

00:03:52.270 --> 00:03:54.975
tools, you should be able to
take those and extend that

00:03:54.975 --> 00:03:55.600
into the cloud.

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:58.630
You no longer to have to break
your data lineage if something

00:03:58.630 --> 00:03:59.990
goes into the cloud.

00:03:59.990 --> 00:04:03.130
Same thing with your security
and compliance models.

00:04:03.130 --> 00:04:05.710
Those are going to
be consistent now

00:04:05.710 --> 00:04:07.690
between on-prem and the cloud.

00:04:07.690 --> 00:04:10.440
So you don't have to necessarily
go through and redevelop

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:13.070
and reaudit everything
for security.

00:04:13.070 --> 00:04:15.570
Now, we're going to talk about
some of the caveats with that

00:04:15.570 --> 00:04:15.870
later.

00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:17.089
Sid's going to address that.

00:04:17.089 --> 00:04:19.547
But in general, you'll be able
to take those same paradigms

00:04:19.547 --> 00:04:20.860
and move those to the cloud.

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:24.340
And of course, all of this is
going to be 100% open source.

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:26.050
Both Google and
Hortonworks are very

00:04:26.050 --> 00:04:30.370
aligned in terms of making sure
that we are contributing back

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:31.810
to the open source community.

00:04:31.810 --> 00:04:35.340
So both the GCS connector
as well as Hortonworks

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:39.730
are committed to making
this 100% open source.

00:04:39.730 --> 00:04:42.100
So before we get to the
new Hortonworks model,

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:46.000
just to level set, I
do want to talk briefly

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:49.030
about something that's existed
in the cloud for a while.

00:04:49.030 --> 00:04:52.570
And this is the
Google Cloud Storage.

00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:57.930
This can be used alongside
HDFS, the Hadoop file system,

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:00.860
to separate your storage
from your computer.

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:03.250
You can make this your
persistent storage layer.

00:05:03.250 --> 00:05:04.900
And the advantage
of this is now,

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:10.730
you can start to scope
your clusters to the job.

00:05:10.730 --> 00:05:13.660
So instead of spending a lot
of time building these Spark

00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:18.250
configurations to send to a
multi-purpose cluster that's

00:05:18.250 --> 00:05:21.310
going to then go compete for
resources, what you can do

00:05:21.310 --> 00:05:22.890
with things, like
Cloud Dataproc,

00:05:22.890 --> 00:05:26.830
is if you have a machine
learning-heavy job, you spin

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:28.190
out a compute heavy cluster.

00:05:28.190 --> 00:05:30.130
Send your machine
learning job to that.

00:05:30.130 --> 00:05:33.910
If you have interactive
analysis job--

00:05:33.910 --> 00:05:37.930
so things like Hive, or you
want to have a BI layer--

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:40.090
that can just be a
memory-heavy cluster,

00:05:40.090 --> 00:05:42.100
and you send jobs to that.

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:43.990
And Dataproc has
a lot of features,

00:05:43.990 --> 00:05:47.230
such as workflow templates,
that will help you build these.

00:05:47.230 --> 00:05:50.736
And we have customers, like
our friends at Spotify labs,

00:05:50.736 --> 00:05:52.360
who actually create
a framework, called

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:54.930
Sypdra, that sits on top
of Dataproc that will also

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:58.510
help you dynamically
size these jobs.

00:05:58.510 --> 00:06:00.430
But with this
Hortonworks partnership,

00:06:00.430 --> 00:06:02.530
what we are really
able to do now

00:06:02.530 --> 00:06:06.190
is we can start to mix
and match entire Hadoop

00:06:06.190 --> 00:06:12.700
distributions, along with
the job sizing as well.

00:06:12.700 --> 00:06:16.490
And so that architecture,
it's really powerful.

00:06:16.490 --> 00:06:18.430
And if you use it
correctly, this

00:06:18.430 --> 00:06:21.480
is also going to be a
big cost saver as well.

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:24.625
So the idea here is
that now, pointing all

00:06:24.625 --> 00:06:28.240
to the same source of
data, your HDP users--

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:30.760
so those folks who have spent
the last five years learning

00:06:30.760 --> 00:06:34.930
Ambari and are happy with it, or
your data analysts that really

00:06:34.930 --> 00:06:37.589
like working in the Hive
web interface with Pez,

00:06:37.589 --> 00:06:39.880
and they want to keep all of
that, all those enterprise

00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:41.260
features--

00:06:41.260 --> 00:06:44.000
they can simply move
that to the cloud.

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:45.820
However, as you are
finding some jobs that

00:06:45.820 --> 00:06:49.030
maybe don't require a lot of
those enterprise features,

00:06:49.030 --> 00:06:50.870
you can spin that.

00:06:50.870 --> 00:06:53.325
Nightly ETL jobs is a
good example of that.

00:06:53.325 --> 00:06:54.700
You could start
to spin those off

00:06:54.700 --> 00:06:58.360
into short-lived Dataproc
clusters and save some money.

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:01.660
Also, because all the compute
and storage is decoupled,

00:07:01.660 --> 00:07:05.555
you can have more
experimental clusters.

00:07:05.555 --> 00:07:07.680
Maybe your data engineer
or your data science team,

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:11.830
they want to start experimenting
with Spark 2.3 on Kubernetes.

00:07:11.830 --> 00:07:13.960
They can still be pointing
the same data sets

00:07:13.960 --> 00:07:18.400
that all your enterprise
HDP users are using.

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:20.800
Now, I think it is worth
taking a quick pause.

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:22.990
Because we talked
a lot about, hey,

00:07:22.990 --> 00:07:29.580
just use these cloud storage
mechanisms instead of HDFS.

00:07:29.580 --> 00:07:33.160
But I think its worth a pause
to talk about what is actually

00:07:33.160 --> 00:07:34.190
happening.

00:07:34.190 --> 00:07:36.490
Because there are
some changes that take

00:07:36.490 --> 00:07:38.812
place, especially in
the security layer,

00:07:38.812 --> 00:07:39.895
as you make these changes.

00:07:39.895 --> 00:07:42.430
And it's worth understanding
what's happening and why.

00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:50.460
The GCS connector, it's
kind of a misnomer.

00:07:50.460 --> 00:07:53.640
But it's not actually
pulling data into HDFS.

00:07:53.640 --> 00:07:56.610
It is, at the block level
and over Google's very high

00:07:56.610 --> 00:08:00.300
performing network, just
directly accessing that data.

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:04.740
And so Hadoop has this abstract
notion of a file system

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:08.310
of which HDFS is just
one implementation.

00:08:08.310 --> 00:08:13.160
You can also use GCS, S3A,
and various other systems.

00:08:13.160 --> 00:08:16.486
This was originally
designed by the folks

00:08:16.486 --> 00:08:17.610
who were developing Hadoop.

00:08:17.610 --> 00:08:20.820
It was pretty fortuitous
the way they did this.

00:08:20.820 --> 00:08:22.470
When you're working
with big data,

00:08:22.470 --> 00:08:24.636
you want to be able to have
block sizes of it, like,

00:08:24.636 --> 00:08:25.710
64 gig.

00:08:25.710 --> 00:08:27.840
But you don't want that
limited just to one

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:29.380
disk on one machine.

00:08:29.380 --> 00:08:30.840
So you extract that away.

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:32.809
You just deal with
the blocks of data.

00:08:32.809 --> 00:08:36.450
And so that works great for
both HDFS as well as GCS

00:08:36.450 --> 00:08:39.870
However, what that does mean
is a lot of that metadata

00:08:39.870 --> 00:08:44.300
information at the file
system level, that's

00:08:44.300 --> 00:08:46.140
net storage subsystem,
and that doesn't

00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:47.670
go into the Hadoop layer.

00:08:47.670 --> 00:08:51.090
So that's important, again, for
some security considerations.

00:08:51.090 --> 00:08:54.180
Meaning you have things,
like Apache Ranger.

00:08:54.180 --> 00:08:57.100
It's not always the easiest
idea, or the best idea

00:08:57.100 --> 00:08:59.820
just to slap that
onto GCS storage,

00:08:59.820 --> 00:09:02.925
because that really
relies on HDFS

00:09:02.925 --> 00:09:06.300
permissions and some
positive compliance things.

00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:09.060
But we definitely do want
to learn more about how

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:12.060
this connector works because
there's a lot of benefits

00:09:12.060 --> 00:09:13.620
to using this storage
connector, even

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:16.660
though some of the
security paradigms change.

00:09:16.660 --> 00:09:18.540
So for instance, as
we just talked about,

00:09:18.540 --> 00:09:20.670
you get that direct data access.

00:09:20.670 --> 00:09:23.930
This is not just
pulling data into HDFS.

00:09:23.930 --> 00:09:26.820
This is actually pulling
blocks of data over Google's

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:28.780
high performance network.

00:09:28.780 --> 00:09:31.470
However, even with that
direct data access,

00:09:31.470 --> 00:09:34.380
you're still going to get
your HDFS compatibility.

00:09:34.380 --> 00:09:36.360
All your jobs are
going to work the same.

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:39.210
You just need to change the
prefix of some of your files

00:09:39.210 --> 00:09:43.330
from HDFS to GS, and you
should be off and running.

00:09:43.330 --> 00:09:44.940
But with that
compatibility, you're

00:09:44.940 --> 00:09:47.520
also going to get a lot
of cloud interoperability.

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:50.780
So it makes it
very easy when you

00:09:50.780 --> 00:09:52.910
have this separated
persistence storage layer

00:09:52.910 --> 00:09:56.770
to move from Hadoop to Spark
to even other Google Cloud

00:09:56.770 --> 00:09:58.170
Services.

00:09:58.170 --> 00:10:00.840
So it's really a great
gateway for getting

00:10:00.840 --> 00:10:03.540
into some of these more
interesting higher level

00:10:03.540 --> 00:10:06.000
Google services, like BigQuery.

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:08.910
So I was working with
a e-commerce company.

00:10:08.910 --> 00:10:12.600
And their pattern is they start
with all of their log data.

00:10:12.600 --> 00:10:14.016
That's sequential output.

00:10:14.016 --> 00:10:16.140
And they would just run
straight MapReduce on that.

00:10:16.140 --> 00:10:19.620
Then that data, they start
to analyze with Spark,

00:10:19.620 --> 00:10:22.224
and turn that into these
business data marts.

00:10:22.224 --> 00:10:23.890
This is mostly from
marketing analytics,

00:10:23.890 --> 00:10:28.100
so it's things like their
campaigns, their per customer

00:10:28.100 --> 00:10:29.400
use of the data.

00:10:29.400 --> 00:10:32.190
And then they store
that in Google BigQuery,

00:10:32.190 --> 00:10:35.075
and that becomes pretty much
their enterprise data warehouse

00:10:35.075 --> 00:10:36.450
where their data
analysts can use

00:10:36.450 --> 00:10:38.280
it to understand
the business better,

00:10:38.280 --> 00:10:43.000
put their Tableau dashboards and
things like that on top of it.

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:45.180
And so this is all possible
because of the data

00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:46.470
accessibility.

00:10:46.470 --> 00:10:50.727
So when you shut down
a Hadoop cluster,

00:10:50.727 --> 00:10:52.560
you're going to lose
access to all that data

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:53.940
that's sitting in HDFS.

00:10:53.940 --> 00:10:56.220
It's really tied to
these spinning disks.

00:10:56.220 --> 00:10:58.470
That's not true with
Google Cloud Storage.

00:10:58.470 --> 00:11:00.660
It's separated, so
your applications

00:11:00.660 --> 00:11:03.870
can still access that data,
even where you Hadoop cluster

00:11:03.870 --> 00:11:05.327
isn't running.

00:11:05.327 --> 00:11:07.160
And of course, even
though it is accessible,

00:11:07.160 --> 00:11:09.940
it's also highly available.

00:11:09.940 --> 00:11:13.560
And this is done without
much of a performance flaw.

00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:15.810
But the really cool thing
about both the accessibility

00:11:15.810 --> 00:11:19.050
and the availability is there's
really no storage management

00:11:19.050 --> 00:11:20.800
overhead on your part.

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:23.490
So anybody that's
administrating HDFS today

00:11:23.490 --> 00:11:27.270
knows the pain points of
running checks on that data,

00:11:27.270 --> 00:11:29.550
upgrading versions,
doing rollbacks.

00:11:29.550 --> 00:11:31.520
That kind of work is done.

00:11:31.520 --> 00:11:33.900
Except with Cloud Storage,
it's done by the Google Site

00:11:33.900 --> 00:11:35.181
Reliability Engineering team.

00:11:35.181 --> 00:11:36.930
It's not something
that your team is going

00:11:36.930 --> 00:11:38.138
to have to carry a pager for.

00:11:38.138 --> 00:11:41.350
So it really helps
with that overhead.

00:11:41.350 --> 00:11:42.990
You're also going to
get quick startup.

00:11:42.990 --> 00:11:45.720
So typically, with
HDFS, you don't

00:11:45.720 --> 00:11:48.780
get to start running jobs
until your name node comes out

00:11:48.780 --> 00:11:50.170
of safe mode.

00:11:50.170 --> 00:11:51.840
With Google Cloud
Storage, as soon

00:11:51.840 --> 00:11:54.510
as your workers come online, you
can start to access that data

00:11:54.510 --> 00:11:56.160
and process that data.

00:11:56.160 --> 00:11:58.650
And a lot of customers that
we're working with, over time,

00:11:58.650 --> 00:12:01.170
have told us that
that's cost saving

00:12:01.170 --> 00:12:03.480
because Google Cloud
is something that

00:12:03.480 --> 00:12:05.850
builds at the per second level.

00:12:05.850 --> 00:12:08.050
So if you're talking
about minutes every time

00:12:08.050 --> 00:12:10.520
a femoral cluster comes
up for a specific job,

00:12:10.520 --> 00:12:13.830
over time, that's going to be
a significant cost reduction

00:12:13.830 --> 00:12:15.270
for that quick startup.

00:12:15.270 --> 00:12:17.940
You're not going to be paying
for the inefficiencies of start

00:12:17.940 --> 00:12:19.290
up costs.

00:12:19.290 --> 00:12:22.020
And then finally, you get
integrated into the Google

00:12:22.020 --> 00:12:23.700
Cloud Security ecosystem.

00:12:23.700 --> 00:12:26.160
And you'll get to take advantage
of all the cloud security.

00:12:26.160 --> 00:12:28.380
But again, I definitely
want to point out

00:12:28.380 --> 00:12:30.210
this is great that
it gets you in there.

00:12:30.210 --> 00:12:35.520
But you want to be aware of
the changes that do occur,

00:12:35.520 --> 00:12:37.724
because they're a different
storage subsystem,

00:12:37.724 --> 00:12:40.140
and you're going to have to
manage these different storage

00:12:40.140 --> 00:12:43.140
subsystems HDFS in
Google Cloud Storage

00:12:43.140 --> 00:12:45.280
a little bit different thing.

00:12:45.280 --> 00:12:48.530
So a couple of quick tips
for working with Google Cloud

00:12:48.530 --> 00:12:49.390
Storage.

00:12:49.390 --> 00:12:51.140
You definitely want
to be taking advantage

00:12:51.140 --> 00:12:53.520
of our different
storage classes of GCS.

00:12:56.390 --> 00:12:58.160
Other object stores
that you might

00:12:58.160 --> 00:13:01.190
be accustomed to working
with, they have separate API

00:13:01.190 --> 00:13:03.310
calls, where if I put
something into an archive

00:13:03.310 --> 00:13:05.870
or cold storage, I have
to call a separate API,

00:13:05.870 --> 00:13:07.640
restore the in four hours.

00:13:07.640 --> 00:13:11.750
All three of the storage
classes are the same API calls,

00:13:11.750 --> 00:13:13.340
same system.

00:13:13.340 --> 00:13:15.890
Your jobs will work the same
between regional storage

00:13:15.890 --> 00:13:17.090
or nearline storage.

00:13:17.090 --> 00:13:19.940
It's just a matter of
where you put them.

00:13:19.940 --> 00:13:23.220
So regional storage, that
comes in two flavors--

00:13:23.220 --> 00:13:26.370
multi-regional or regional-- and
this is where most of your data

00:13:26.370 --> 00:13:27.220
is going to live.

00:13:27.220 --> 00:13:29.636
This is what you're going to
use for your interactive Hive

00:13:29.636 --> 00:13:30.562
or Spark analysis.

00:13:30.562 --> 00:13:32.270
And the rule of thumb
here is, basically,

00:13:32.270 --> 00:13:35.990
any batch job that will
occur more than once a month.

00:13:35.990 --> 00:13:38.690
Now nearline storage
is the next tier down.

00:13:38.690 --> 00:13:40.219
And this is really
for batch jobs

00:13:40.219 --> 00:13:42.260
that you're going to use
for historical reporting

00:13:42.260 --> 00:13:43.227
or aggregation.

00:13:43.227 --> 00:13:44.810
Rule of thumb here
is, if you're going

00:13:44.810 --> 00:13:46.830
to touch data
about once a month,

00:13:46.830 --> 00:13:48.620
this is nearline storage.

00:13:48.620 --> 00:13:50.090
And then finally,
coldline storage

00:13:50.090 --> 00:13:52.735
comes into place for that
post-process data, stuff

00:13:52.735 --> 00:13:54.860
you probably don't think
you're going to use again.

00:13:54.860 --> 00:13:56.960
But you want to keep
it around just in case,

00:13:56.960 --> 00:13:59.335
or maybe you have compliance
reasons to keep it around.

00:13:59.335 --> 00:14:00.710
Rule of thumb
there, if you think

00:14:00.710 --> 00:14:03.820
you're going to touch it once
a year or less, coldline.

00:14:03.820 --> 00:14:06.980
Now, next tip is,
don't be afraid to use

00:14:06.980 --> 00:14:09.210
GCS as a destination.

00:14:09.210 --> 00:14:12.050
So GCS-- Google Cloud Storage--

00:14:12.050 --> 00:14:14.000
we provide strong
global consistency

00:14:14.000 --> 00:14:16.670
for a lot of those operations
that are relevant to Hadoop--

00:14:16.670 --> 00:14:18.570
read after write, read
after metadata update,

00:14:18.570 --> 00:14:20.750
read after delete, bucket
listing, object listing.

00:14:20.750 --> 00:14:24.460
So some other object store
you may have worked with,

00:14:24.460 --> 00:14:26.150
they're eventually consistent.

00:14:26.150 --> 00:14:27.680
So to get around
that with Hadoop,

00:14:27.680 --> 00:14:30.010
you have to build some kind
of glitchy architectures,

00:14:30.010 --> 00:14:31.760
where you might have
a NoSQL database that

00:14:31.760 --> 00:14:35.716
sits in between the object
store and Hadoop that's

00:14:35.716 --> 00:14:38.090
going to keep track of what
the files are actually doing,

00:14:38.090 --> 00:14:39.170
what's available.

00:14:39.170 --> 00:14:42.020
For the most part, that
all goes away with GCS.

00:14:42.020 --> 00:14:44.430
You can just use it
as the destination.

00:14:44.430 --> 00:14:46.340
Now there's a couple
of outlier cases

00:14:46.340 --> 00:14:49.610
there, such as a
rename [INAUDIBLE]..

00:14:49.610 --> 00:14:52.500
But what our customers have
told us that for the most part,

00:14:52.500 --> 00:14:53.312
it works fine.

00:14:53.312 --> 00:14:54.770
For the vast majority
of use cases,

00:14:54.770 --> 00:14:57.570
they use GCS as a destination.

00:14:57.570 --> 00:15:00.570
So if you want to get
started with this,

00:15:00.570 --> 00:15:03.957
you can try it in pretty much
any Apache, Hadoop system

00:15:03.957 --> 00:15:04.540
that you have.

00:15:04.540 --> 00:15:07.070
All you have to do is
drop a single JAR file

00:15:07.070 --> 00:15:08.670
into the classpath.

00:15:08.670 --> 00:15:11.390
And you can pull that JAR
right off of our GitHub repo.

00:15:11.390 --> 00:15:12.770
Starting to release 1.9.

00:15:12.770 --> 00:15:17.690
It's going to work with
both Hadoop 2.0 and 3.0.

00:15:17.690 --> 00:15:20.150
And then you just have to
configure the permissions.

00:15:20.150 --> 00:15:23.930
So if you're in Google Cloud
Storage or Google Cloud itself,

00:15:23.930 --> 00:15:26.570
definitely take advantage
of service account for this.

00:15:26.570 --> 00:15:29.990
That will give you that
transparent token exchange.

00:15:29.990 --> 00:15:34.400
But if you're coming from
on-prem or a different cloud,

00:15:34.400 --> 00:15:37.040
go ahead and you can
set those credentials

00:15:37.040 --> 00:15:38.476
in the core-site file.

00:15:38.476 --> 00:15:40.100
So with that, I'm
going to hand it over

00:15:40.100 --> 00:15:41.870
to Sid, who's going to talk
more about hybrid environments

00:15:41.870 --> 00:15:43.486
and enterprise security.

00:15:48.722 --> 00:15:50.949
SIDDARTH SETH: Thanks, Chris.

00:15:50.949 --> 00:15:51.740
I'm Siddharth Seth.

00:15:51.740 --> 00:15:54.540
I work as an engineer
at Hortonworks.

00:15:54.540 --> 00:15:57.430
I've been associated with a
bunch of Hadoop projects--

00:15:57.430 --> 00:16:02.192
Hive, [INAUDIBLE],, YARN, MLApp,
for about seven years now.

00:16:02.192 --> 00:16:03.650
So before getting
started, I'd just

00:16:03.650 --> 00:16:07.040
like to set some context
on hybrid environments

00:16:07.040 --> 00:16:12.830
as well as security, which will
be useful later on in the talk.

00:16:12.830 --> 00:16:15.860
So first off is the
Hortonworks Data Plane service.

00:16:15.860 --> 00:16:19.320
This service lets you
manage your on-prem as well

00:16:19.320 --> 00:16:22.210
as cloud clusters in
a single environment.

00:16:22.210 --> 00:16:24.085
It gives you a view of
all of these clusters.

00:16:26.780 --> 00:16:29.450
The Data Plane
platform itself lets

00:16:29.450 --> 00:16:31.310
you run services on top of it.

00:16:31.310 --> 00:16:34.400
And that's really where
you get the value.

00:16:34.400 --> 00:16:36.860
Some of these services are
the Data Steward Studio,

00:16:36.860 --> 00:16:40.400
the Data Lifecycle Manager,
which are already available.

00:16:40.400 --> 00:16:43.160
And what's coming soon is
the Data Analytics Studio,

00:16:43.160 --> 00:16:43.790
Cloudbreak.

00:16:43.790 --> 00:16:47.480
And it's possible for
additional services

00:16:47.480 --> 00:16:49.050
to be added to
the same platform.

00:16:49.050 --> 00:16:51.830
The Data Plane platform
provides an interface

00:16:51.830 --> 00:16:55.919
to access these clusters as
well as user management and role

00:16:55.919 --> 00:16:57.085
management for the services.

00:16:59.870 --> 00:17:03.540
So DSS and DLM are two services
which I'll talk about today.

00:17:03.540 --> 00:17:06.510
DSS lets you manage your
data sets, whether they are

00:17:06.510 --> 00:17:10.869
on-prem or on cloud storage.

00:17:10.869 --> 00:17:12.540
You can view the data sets.

00:17:12.540 --> 00:17:15.000
You can view access patterns.

00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:18.240
It comes with a view which lets
you look at audit information

00:17:18.240 --> 00:17:23.750
to see who has accessed
the patterns on the data.

00:17:23.750 --> 00:17:26.589
It also comes with
a few profilers.

00:17:26.589 --> 00:17:31.990
A very interesting one is
sensitive data, or PII data,

00:17:31.990 --> 00:17:34.020
profiler which can scan
through your data sets.

00:17:36.640 --> 00:17:39.220
Essentially, it can go
and tag sensitive data

00:17:39.220 --> 00:17:43.512
at a column level or at
a data set level, which

00:17:43.512 --> 00:17:45.470
then integrates later on
with Atlas and Ranger,

00:17:45.470 --> 00:17:48.055
and we'll come to that.

00:17:48.055 --> 00:17:52.680
DLM is the data
lifecycle manager.

00:17:58.660 --> 00:18:01.390
It's responsible for data
and metadata replication.

00:18:01.390 --> 00:18:04.290
So it lets you replicate data
between on-prem as well as

00:18:04.290 --> 00:18:05.710
cloud environments.

00:18:05.710 --> 00:18:10.110
It also serves as a
disaster recovery solution.

00:18:10.110 --> 00:18:13.240
Because it's replicating
both the data,

00:18:13.240 --> 00:18:16.330
metadata associated with, let's
say [INAUDIBLE],, as well as

00:18:16.330 --> 00:18:19.660
your Ranger or audit policies
or any security policies

00:18:19.660 --> 00:18:21.630
which you have in Ranger.

00:18:24.690 --> 00:18:28.590
It's great that we can
run Hadoop clusters.

00:18:28.590 --> 00:18:30.570
If you look at very
simple Hadoop clusters,

00:18:30.570 --> 00:18:33.000
you have storage,
and you have compute.

00:18:33.000 --> 00:18:35.864
Very often, that's not
enough for enterprises.

00:18:35.864 --> 00:18:37.530
It's great that they
can run their jobs.

00:18:37.530 --> 00:18:40.890
But enterprises require
governance and security

00:18:40.890 --> 00:18:42.692
in place.

00:18:42.692 --> 00:18:44.400
Towards this, there's
several components.

00:18:44.400 --> 00:18:48.336
There's Knox, which serves
as perimeter security.

00:18:48.336 --> 00:18:49.710
Especially in
cloud environments,

00:18:49.710 --> 00:18:52.970
you don't necessarily want
to expose all of your points

00:18:52.970 --> 00:18:54.810
over the public cloud.

00:18:54.810 --> 00:18:58.170
Knox secures all of
these public end points.

00:18:58.170 --> 00:19:01.620
The Hive metastore, of course,
has metadata about your tables

00:19:01.620 --> 00:19:03.832
and data, in general.

00:19:03.832 --> 00:19:06.165
And we'll talk a little bit
more about Ranger and Atlas.

00:19:10.135 --> 00:19:12.260
For people who haven't used
these products already,

00:19:12.260 --> 00:19:17.700
Ranger gives you authorization
policies for your data.

00:19:17.700 --> 00:19:19.800
So this can be, let's
say, at a database level,

00:19:19.800 --> 00:19:21.390
at a table level.

00:19:21.390 --> 00:19:23.690
In addition to that,
what's very important

00:19:23.690 --> 00:19:25.850
here is it gives you
fine-grained access control.

00:19:25.850 --> 00:19:30.230
So you can actually go and
say that a certain column is

00:19:30.230 --> 00:19:32.780
sensitive data, flag
that, and set up

00:19:32.780 --> 00:19:35.720
a policy to say that
only certain individuals

00:19:35.720 --> 00:19:38.840
or certain organizations
have access to this data.

00:19:38.840 --> 00:19:41.220
If someone else were to
try to access the data,

00:19:41.220 --> 00:19:45.020
the data ends up getting lost
while running a Hive query.

00:19:45.020 --> 00:19:47.087
Another piece over here
is tag-based policies.

00:19:47.087 --> 00:19:49.670
And we'll come to that when we
put all these systems together.

00:19:52.940 --> 00:19:55.340
Apache Atlas is for governance.

00:19:55.340 --> 00:19:58.910
So this keeps track
of data lineage

00:19:58.910 --> 00:20:01.910
as well as data classification.

00:20:01.910 --> 00:20:03.980
So when you generate
a new data set,

00:20:03.980 --> 00:20:06.175
you'll be able to say
what the source data

00:20:06.175 --> 00:20:09.650
sets were to get to this data.

00:20:09.650 --> 00:20:12.230
That information
is always tracked.

00:20:12.230 --> 00:20:16.060
Atlas also is integrated
within most parts of the Hadoop

00:20:16.060 --> 00:20:17.370
ecosystem.

00:20:17.370 --> 00:20:21.322
And it has APIs for integrating
with external components.

00:20:21.322 --> 00:20:23.030
In case you have an
external data source,

00:20:23.030 --> 00:20:24.488
and you're bringing
data from that,

00:20:24.488 --> 00:20:27.380
it has APIs to
integrate with that.

00:20:27.380 --> 00:20:29.270
Now, putting all
of this together--

00:20:32.370 --> 00:20:35.570
DSS, Ranger, and
Atlas-- you could end up

00:20:35.570 --> 00:20:40.020
saying the DSS sensitive
data profiler runs

00:20:40.020 --> 00:20:44.830
to tag sensitive data on
all of your data sets.

00:20:44.830 --> 00:20:48.250
That then sets up tags in
Atlas, or you could manually

00:20:48.250 --> 00:20:49.846
set up tags in Atlas.

00:20:49.846 --> 00:20:51.720
And after that, you set
up policies in Ranger

00:20:51.720 --> 00:20:54.760
saying that any data
with this specific tag

00:20:54.760 --> 00:20:59.810
is only accessible to users who
are authorized to use the data.

00:20:59.810 --> 00:21:02.590
And this provides a
very powerful attraction

00:21:02.590 --> 00:21:07.280
for the entire security
mechanism of securing your data

00:21:07.280 --> 00:21:09.190
and making sure you
have an audit trail.

00:21:09.190 --> 00:21:13.630
Ranger takes care of the audit
for all of your data access.

00:21:13.630 --> 00:21:15.550
So that results in that
context for the rest

00:21:15.550 --> 00:21:17.410
of the presentation.

00:21:17.410 --> 00:21:19.210
So deployment
architectures of the cloud

00:21:19.210 --> 00:21:21.520
is what we'll talk about next.

00:21:21.520 --> 00:21:25.460
This is a diagram of your
typical on-prem deployment.

00:21:25.460 --> 00:21:28.180
Now, these are typically
long-running clusters.

00:21:28.180 --> 00:21:31.810
They are shared
by multiple users.

00:21:31.810 --> 00:21:35.830
Resource management is
handled by YARN queues.

00:21:35.830 --> 00:21:39.260
Different organizations can end
up sharing the same cluster.

00:21:39.260 --> 00:21:41.790
They'll have allocations
within YARN queues.

00:21:41.790 --> 00:21:44.980
This cluster is secure,
will typically be secure.

00:21:44.980 --> 00:21:47.830
You'll have authentication in
place that's like your l-dot.

00:21:47.830 --> 00:21:49.690
Kerberos will be in place.

00:21:49.690 --> 00:21:51.590
Data sets in HDFS.

00:21:51.590 --> 00:21:54.460
Temporary data is called
out separately over here.

00:21:54.460 --> 00:21:55.700
It sits on the same HDFS.

00:21:55.700 --> 00:21:57.366
It's called out
separately because we're

00:21:57.366 --> 00:21:59.740
going to iterate on
the site further down.

00:21:59.740 --> 00:22:00.580
There's a metastore.

00:22:00.580 --> 00:22:02.916
There's Ranger, Atlas.

00:22:02.916 --> 00:22:04.540
Atlas isn't explicitly
called out here.

00:22:04.540 --> 00:22:06.910
But there's Ranger, Atlas
in place for your security

00:22:06.910 --> 00:22:10.010
and governance.

00:22:10.010 --> 00:22:14.000
Very important here is also that
this cluster is long-running.

00:22:14.000 --> 00:22:15.920
It needs to be reliable.

00:22:15.920 --> 00:22:19.167
All the musters are
highly available.

00:22:19.167 --> 00:22:21.500
This is a simplification of
the same diagram which we'll

00:22:21.500 --> 00:22:23.215
iterate upon further down.

00:22:23.215 --> 00:22:25.960
Again, data and temp data
here are the same HDFS.

00:22:25.960 --> 00:22:29.300
They're just called out, so that
we can iterate on this diagram.

00:22:31.840 --> 00:22:35.000
As a first step, it's very
tempting to kind of say, OK,

00:22:35.000 --> 00:22:36.620
I have an architecture on-prem.

00:22:36.620 --> 00:22:38.620
Let me just take the same
thing-- previsions VMs

00:22:38.620 --> 00:22:41.560
on the cloud and run the same
architecture on the cloud

00:22:41.560 --> 00:22:43.510
with data setting on HDFS.

00:22:43.510 --> 00:22:46.960
Now while, in theory, it's
simple to reason about,

00:22:46.960 --> 00:22:50.620
it's not really the
best way forward.

00:22:50.620 --> 00:22:54.790
The main problems here are,
one, is data setting on HDFS?

00:22:54.790 --> 00:23:01.990
Which means you can't really
scale your cluster that way.

00:23:01.990 --> 00:23:05.440
When HDFS is replicated,
if you remove enough nodes,

00:23:05.440 --> 00:23:07.570
you will end up losing data.

00:23:07.570 --> 00:23:09.640
Ultimately, you can
use persistent disks,

00:23:09.640 --> 00:23:11.800
and then you'll have to
deal with reattaching disks

00:23:11.800 --> 00:23:14.210
when you scale up or
scale down the cluster.

00:23:14.210 --> 00:23:17.254
So it's not necessarily
the best mechanism to use

00:23:17.254 --> 00:23:18.420
when migrating to the cloud.

00:23:21.566 --> 00:23:23.440
All right, moving on to
some cloud deployment

00:23:23.440 --> 00:23:25.104
portals which can be used.

00:23:25.104 --> 00:23:27.270
You need to start thinking
a little differently when

00:23:27.270 --> 00:23:29.440
moving from one kind of cloud.

00:23:29.440 --> 00:23:32.410
All of these deployment
architectures

00:23:32.410 --> 00:23:35.200
serve different use
cases and can be used.

00:23:35.200 --> 00:23:38.270
The first one here
is very simple--

00:23:38.270 --> 00:23:40.950
an ephemeral cluster.

00:23:40.950 --> 00:23:44.900
The pieces in the red outline
are optional components.

00:23:44.900 --> 00:23:48.070
So your first step is to say,
I'm going to have compute.

00:23:48.070 --> 00:23:53.800
I have a temporary HDFS only for
temporary data on the cluster.

00:23:53.800 --> 00:23:57.010
And the main storage
goes to GCS over here.

00:23:57.010 --> 00:24:00.000
And then this system,
Hadoop security is optional.

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:03.110
You can, instead, rely
upon cloud security, which

00:24:03.110 --> 00:24:06.680
includes IM network
security rules to make sure

00:24:06.680 --> 00:24:09.390
only certain people have
access to the system.

00:24:09.390 --> 00:24:10.760
There's no metadata.

00:24:13.410 --> 00:24:17.040
When you get started, you
can attach a metastore to it

00:24:17.040 --> 00:24:18.060
if you want.

00:24:18.060 --> 00:24:20.590
You can attach Ranger
policies at a later point.

00:24:20.590 --> 00:24:24.076
But you end up redefining the
same thing over and over again.

00:24:24.076 --> 00:24:25.200
It's great for ad-hoc jobs.

00:24:27.870 --> 00:24:30.760
Moving on is where we
introduce shared services.

00:24:30.760 --> 00:24:33.360
So at this point, what we're
doing is we take the metadata,

00:24:33.360 --> 00:24:38.497
and we split the cluster data
into two different entities.

00:24:38.497 --> 00:24:40.830
We have a shared services
cluster, which, at this point,

00:24:40.830 --> 00:24:43.010
becomes a long-running system.

00:24:43.010 --> 00:24:44.670
It's always on.

00:24:44.670 --> 00:24:48.060
Other clusters will be launched
and then attached to this.

00:24:48.060 --> 00:24:51.190
Again, in this model,
Hadoop security is optional.

00:24:51.190 --> 00:24:54.300
It's very similar
to the previous one.

00:24:54.300 --> 00:24:56.650
The metadata sits in GCS.

00:24:56.650 --> 00:24:59.340
The advantage we get over here
is that the metadata is always

00:24:59.340 --> 00:24:59.880
available.

00:24:59.880 --> 00:25:03.180
It's easy to discover data.

00:25:03.180 --> 00:25:05.410
You can attach as many
clusters as you want.

00:25:05.410 --> 00:25:08.055
And all of them will
see all of the metadata

00:25:08.055 --> 00:25:11.710
and will be able to access
the data via GCS itself.

00:25:11.710 --> 00:25:13.630
If you do want authorization,
you will, again,

00:25:13.630 --> 00:25:17.090
define a new Ranger and Atlas.

00:25:17.090 --> 00:25:18.460
And you will connect to that.

00:25:18.460 --> 00:25:20.293
But you will have to
redefine your policies.

00:25:23.360 --> 00:25:26.450
Now this is a system
which works really well.

00:25:26.450 --> 00:25:29.390
A use case of this would be
development of environments.

00:25:29.390 --> 00:25:31.690
As an example, in
Hortonworks, this

00:25:31.690 --> 00:25:34.400
is used extensively,
where we've pre-generated

00:25:34.400 --> 00:25:36.590
some data sets on GCS.

00:25:36.590 --> 00:25:39.530
The metadata is
sitting in cloud SQL.

00:25:39.530 --> 00:25:42.130
And we attached a
metastore to it.

00:25:42.130 --> 00:25:44.470
And whenever we need
to try out new code,

00:25:44.470 --> 00:25:47.780
do some testing, et cetera,
we spin out a new cluster,

00:25:47.780 --> 00:25:50.150
do the testing, do
the experiments,

00:25:50.150 --> 00:25:54.005
tear it down to save costs.

00:25:54.005 --> 00:25:55.380
So taking this to
the next level,

00:25:55.380 --> 00:25:58.500
then, this is where we get into
the enterprise security space.

00:25:58.500 --> 00:26:01.020
We're going to move Ranger
also into this shared services

00:26:01.020 --> 00:26:02.290
cluster as well as Atlas.

00:26:02.290 --> 00:26:03.960
Again, it's not
called out over here.

00:26:03.960 --> 00:26:07.200
But Ranger and Atlas now moving
to the shared services cluster.

00:26:07.200 --> 00:26:09.680
It's long-running.

00:26:09.680 --> 00:26:11.970
A very important
distinction here

00:26:11.970 --> 00:26:15.242
is that Hadoop's security
becomes an important aspect.

00:26:15.242 --> 00:26:17.700
There's not a lot of point in
doing authorization if you're

00:26:17.700 --> 00:26:21.150
not authenticating the users.

00:26:21.150 --> 00:26:22.710
So data sits in GCS.

00:26:22.710 --> 00:26:24.780
Connect as many
clusters as you want.

00:26:24.780 --> 00:26:25.900
Spin up new clusters.

00:26:25.900 --> 00:26:27.850
They'll have their
policies available.

00:26:27.850 --> 00:26:29.820
They'll have the
metadata available.

00:26:29.820 --> 00:26:31.980
All the performance
optimizations

00:26:31.980 --> 00:26:34.260
which come with
the metadata will

00:26:34.260 --> 00:26:36.090
be available on this cluster.

00:26:41.940 --> 00:26:43.781
OK, so just a recap of
the shared services.

00:26:43.781 --> 00:26:45.280
This is essentially
what your system

00:26:45.280 --> 00:26:46.834
will end up looking like.

00:26:46.834 --> 00:26:49.000
There's going to be one
long-running shared services

00:26:49.000 --> 00:26:49.500
cluster.

00:26:51.760 --> 00:26:53.600
It's plugged in with your l-dot.

00:26:53.600 --> 00:26:55.690
It's plugged in with Kerberos.

00:26:55.690 --> 00:26:57.580
You can attach as many
ephemeral clusters

00:26:57.580 --> 00:26:59.600
as you want to
launch jobs, or you

00:26:59.600 --> 00:27:02.680
can have long-running clusters
utilizing the same metadata,

00:27:02.680 --> 00:27:05.000
same policies.

00:27:05.000 --> 00:27:06.910
Long-running cluster,
this is completely up

00:27:06.910 --> 00:27:10.540
to the way organizations want to
structure their Hadoop systems.

00:27:10.540 --> 00:27:12.040
An example of a
long-running cluster

00:27:12.040 --> 00:27:16.090
would be an analytic cluster
which is serving dashboards.

00:27:16.090 --> 00:27:18.160
You don't necessarily
want to spin that down,

00:27:18.160 --> 00:27:20.210
because it's
serving a dashboard.

00:27:20.210 --> 00:27:21.880
So you just attach
the same cluster,

00:27:21.880 --> 00:27:24.460
have your policy enforcement,
have you metadata, all of that

00:27:24.460 --> 00:27:26.650
managed via this.

00:27:26.650 --> 00:27:30.630
Now this is where you really
get the agility of the cloud

00:27:30.630 --> 00:27:32.210
while you get
capacity on demand.

00:27:32.210 --> 00:27:34.080
So if on-prem, for
instance, you've

00:27:34.080 --> 00:27:38.260
run out of capacity on
your Hadoop deployment,

00:27:38.260 --> 00:27:41.590
it takes a long time to
get new servers in place.

00:27:41.590 --> 00:27:45.640
A lot of organizations don't
even want to get new servers.

00:27:45.640 --> 00:27:49.480
You would just provision a few
more instances on Google Cloud,

00:27:49.480 --> 00:27:51.550
and you have new capacity.

00:27:51.550 --> 00:27:54.880
The other big advantage here
is you can leave your existing

00:27:54.880 --> 00:27:56.530
workloads running.

00:27:56.530 --> 00:27:58.210
You may have pipelines
already in place.

00:27:58.210 --> 00:28:01.120
Some stable workloads
are on a schedule.

00:28:01.120 --> 00:28:03.550
But you want to try out
newer versions of software.

00:28:03.550 --> 00:28:05.800
You may have Spark 2.3, or
a newer version of Hive,

00:28:05.800 --> 00:28:07.510
newer version of Spark.

00:28:07.510 --> 00:28:09.460
You can just launch
a separate cluster,

00:28:09.460 --> 00:28:13.019
which will attach, again, to the
same shared services cluster.

00:28:13.019 --> 00:28:13.560
Play with it.

00:28:13.560 --> 00:28:17.920
Once you are happy with it,
you will migrate the production

00:28:17.920 --> 00:28:20.330
environment over to this.

00:28:20.330 --> 00:28:24.930
Similarly, you can set up test
instances against the same.

00:28:24.930 --> 00:28:28.580
Another paradigm is lift
and shift for data on GCS.

00:28:28.580 --> 00:28:33.220
This is a very feasible option.

00:28:33.220 --> 00:28:36.770
Something you should
keep in mind--

00:28:36.770 --> 00:28:39.410
there's very few HDFS nodes,
so it's easy to scale.

00:28:39.410 --> 00:28:42.460
The other thing to keep in mind
over is if it's multi-tenet,

00:28:42.460 --> 00:28:46.100
a single long-running job can
prevent scaling of the cluster

00:28:46.100 --> 00:28:50.200
because there is intermittent
data which cannot be lost.

00:28:50.200 --> 00:28:55.270
Otherwise, a job will have
to end up [INAUDIBLE] status.

00:28:55.270 --> 00:28:57.946
OK, so this talks
about the cluster

00:28:57.946 --> 00:28:59.445
shapes, irrespective
of whether it's

00:28:59.445 --> 00:29:01.278
a shared service cluster,
ephemeral cluster,

00:29:01.278 --> 00:29:04.030
or long-running clusters.

00:29:04.030 --> 00:29:05.510
There's master nodes.

00:29:05.510 --> 00:29:08.870
There are nodes which run
a temporarily HDFS as well

00:29:08.870 --> 00:29:10.940
as double S compute.

00:29:10.940 --> 00:29:12.830
And then there are
compute-only nodes.

00:29:12.830 --> 00:29:14.330
Depending on the
kind of performance

00:29:14.330 --> 00:29:15.913
you want for your
workloads, you would

00:29:15.913 --> 00:29:18.620
run all of these on
non-preemptible VMs.

00:29:18.620 --> 00:29:21.160
And to save costs,
you can always

00:29:21.160 --> 00:29:25.150
wrong the compute-only nodes
on initial preemptible VMs.

00:29:25.150 --> 00:29:28.651
Hadoop knows how to recover
jobs in case nodes go away.

00:29:28.651 --> 00:29:30.525
And it will take care
of all of this for you.

00:29:33.060 --> 00:29:37.210
This talks about the
various Google Cloud

00:29:37.210 --> 00:29:39.790
components which are used.

00:29:39.790 --> 00:29:44.590
HDP, HDF will run on
Google Compute Engine.

00:29:44.590 --> 00:29:48.620
Ingest-- you will bring
your data into GCS.

00:29:48.620 --> 00:29:50.770
You write your data out to GCS.

00:29:50.770 --> 00:29:53.640
The shared services
cluster, you use Cloud SQL

00:29:53.640 --> 00:29:55.615
for Ranger and the metastore.

00:29:55.615 --> 00:29:57.240
And the shared services
cluster, again,

00:29:57.240 --> 00:29:59.797
runs on Google Compute Engine.

00:29:59.797 --> 00:30:02.130
Logging and monitoring can
be integrated into the shared

00:30:02.130 --> 00:30:03.594
services cluster.

00:30:03.594 --> 00:30:05.010
Part of the logging
and monitoring

00:30:05.010 --> 00:30:09.400
would go in over here, where
we have metrics via Ambari.

00:30:09.400 --> 00:30:12.440
The other part-- systems
logs-- could go in

00:30:12.440 --> 00:30:17.842
through Google Cloud
logging infrastructure.

00:30:21.220 --> 00:30:23.290
All right, so covering
some recommendations

00:30:23.290 --> 00:30:27.360
which are very specific
to Google Cloud.

00:30:27.360 --> 00:30:29.580
Something to keep in mind
is that this performance

00:30:29.580 --> 00:30:32.840
scales with the size of the
disk up to a certain point.

00:30:32.840 --> 00:30:36.270
So size your disks accordingly.

00:30:36.270 --> 00:30:38.970
Also very important
is attached disks.

00:30:38.970 --> 00:30:42.000
Google offers local SSDs and
a couple of attached disks,

00:30:42.000 --> 00:30:44.030
either spindles or SSDs.

00:30:44.030 --> 00:30:46.707
But attached disks count
toward network traffic.

00:30:46.707 --> 00:30:48.290
In fact, they count
towards, depending

00:30:48.290 --> 00:30:51.380
on the type of
attached disks, it

00:30:51.380 --> 00:30:54.920
will either be 3x of the actual
data being written or 6x,

00:30:54.920 --> 00:30:57.032
depending on whether it's
[INAUDIBLE] original.

00:30:57.032 --> 00:30:58.490
So that's something
to be aware of.

00:30:58.490 --> 00:31:00.502
And this will compete
with the actual jobs

00:31:00.502 --> 00:31:01.460
running on the cluster.

00:31:06.624 --> 00:31:08.040
Shuffle data is,
essentially, data

00:31:08.040 --> 00:31:14.440
which is transferred between
tasks during a running job.

00:31:14.440 --> 00:31:17.350
This is data which, if it's
lost, it doesn't matter.

00:31:17.350 --> 00:31:19.930
Because again,
Hadoop run times know

00:31:19.930 --> 00:31:23.240
how to regenerate this data.

00:31:23.240 --> 00:31:25.610
If this data is small
enough, for best performance,

00:31:25.610 --> 00:31:27.660
locally attached SSDs
is the way to go,

00:31:27.660 --> 00:31:30.710
because you would not be
competing on the network

00:31:30.710 --> 00:31:31.820
bandwidth.

00:31:31.820 --> 00:31:34.820
This data is typically for a
job transferred, in any case,

00:31:34.820 --> 00:31:35.470
between nodes.

00:31:35.470 --> 00:31:40.794
And there's no real reason to
put it onto attached disks.

00:31:40.794 --> 00:31:43.210
Another important point is to
configure independent disks.

00:31:43.210 --> 00:31:46.720
Because if a certain
disk goes bad,

00:31:46.720 --> 00:31:49.090
Hadoop knows to just remove
it and continue running

00:31:49.090 --> 00:31:51.350
with the remaining disks.

00:31:51.350 --> 00:31:54.630
Temporary HDFS could be a few
standard persistent disks.

00:31:54.630 --> 00:31:55.650
Again, it's temporary.

00:31:55.650 --> 00:31:57.020
You don't really care too much.

00:31:57.020 --> 00:32:00.710
All it's got is crash
directories, intermittent data,

00:32:00.710 --> 00:32:03.970
things like that.

00:32:03.970 --> 00:32:11.640
LLAP he provides interactive
performance with Hive.

00:32:11.640 --> 00:32:12.962
It knows how to cache data.

00:32:12.962 --> 00:32:14.420
It caches data at
the column level.

00:32:14.420 --> 00:32:16.750
It doesn't cache entire files.

00:32:16.750 --> 00:32:19.480
It can either do this
in memory or on disk.

00:32:19.480 --> 00:32:21.195
So given the performance
characteristics

00:32:21.195 --> 00:32:25.030
of the various disks available,
local SSDs is the way to go.

00:32:25.030 --> 00:32:27.600
The one difference here is
combine these SSDs together,

00:32:27.600 --> 00:32:30.420
because it will work
with only a single disk.

00:32:30.420 --> 00:32:32.310
And combine them
together and configure

00:32:32.310 --> 00:32:33.930
LLAP to use that as the cache.

00:32:33.930 --> 00:32:36.300
And this can make a pretty
big performance difference

00:32:36.300 --> 00:32:36.930
when running.

00:32:36.930 --> 00:32:40.320
Because it's got
the data already

00:32:40.320 --> 00:32:41.670
fetched from cloud storage.

00:32:41.670 --> 00:32:44.370
It's got the specific
columns pre-fetched.

00:32:44.370 --> 00:32:48.970
It's got metadata about the
information in the cache.

00:32:48.970 --> 00:32:52.570
GCS is running in
the same region.

00:32:52.570 --> 00:32:56.200
That's pretty much the most
important thing over here.

00:32:56.200 --> 00:32:58.180
You don't necessarily
need to run it.

00:32:58.180 --> 00:33:03.290
You don't want to be accessing
data across regions with GCS.

00:33:03.290 --> 00:33:07.220
In terms of the
network configuration,

00:33:07.220 --> 00:33:10.880
the bandwidth on a node is
linked to the number of cores.

00:33:10.880 --> 00:33:13.757
So that's something to keep in
mind while sizing your nodes.

00:33:13.757 --> 00:33:15.590
The workers typically
need higher bandwidth,

00:33:15.590 --> 00:33:19.716
so you would want
more CPUs over there.

00:33:19.716 --> 00:33:21.590
We already covered that
persistent disk count

00:33:21.590 --> 00:33:22.673
towards the network limit.

00:33:22.673 --> 00:33:26.820
So configure things accordingly.

00:33:26.820 --> 00:33:29.840
Google offers a standard network
tier as well as a premium tier.

00:33:29.840 --> 00:33:31.650
For Hadoop profiles,
most of the traffic

00:33:31.650 --> 00:33:33.960
is within the cluster itself.

00:33:33.960 --> 00:33:36.870
The standard network tier
is more than adequate.

00:33:36.870 --> 00:33:39.960
And very important is
you use private IPs

00:33:39.960 --> 00:33:41.930
within cluster traffic.

00:33:41.930 --> 00:33:44.280
You do not want this
going to public IPs.

00:33:44.280 --> 00:33:45.530
One, it's an extra hub.

00:33:45.530 --> 00:33:46.590
Two, it also costs more.

00:33:49.990 --> 00:33:52.060
In terms of node
sizing, this is not

00:33:52.060 --> 00:33:53.414
very specific recommendations.

00:33:53.414 --> 00:33:55.330
There's plenty of
information available on how

00:33:55.330 --> 00:33:57.210
to size your nodes.

00:33:57.210 --> 00:34:02.110
This just talks about the
flexibility on Google Cloud.

00:34:02.110 --> 00:34:08.940
So one thing to keep in mind
is that the cost for CPUs

00:34:08.940 --> 00:34:11.350
scales linearly.

00:34:11.350 --> 00:34:14.318
So you can size your
nodes accordingly.

00:34:14.318 --> 00:34:15.276
There's high mem nodes.

00:34:15.276 --> 00:34:17.842
There's dedicated
nodes, if required.

00:34:17.842 --> 00:34:20.050
The other bit here is
sustained use and committed use

00:34:20.050 --> 00:34:20.550
discounts.

00:34:20.550 --> 00:34:22.859
Now the committed use discount
here is very important.

00:34:25.370 --> 00:34:29.130
So how it works in Google is
you pay up front for CPU cores

00:34:29.130 --> 00:34:31.810
rather than instant types.

00:34:31.810 --> 00:34:34.996
And at a later point, you can
decide how to use these cores.

00:34:34.996 --> 00:34:36.620
You can define
different instance types

00:34:36.620 --> 00:34:39.320
based on these cores.

00:34:39.320 --> 00:34:41.929
Master nodes, especially
in the ephemeral clusters,

00:34:41.929 --> 00:34:44.250
do not need to be the
biggest nodes in the cluster.

00:34:44.250 --> 00:34:45.940
You don't need fewer components.

00:34:45.940 --> 00:34:50.740
It's absolutely fine
to size them smaller.

00:34:50.740 --> 00:34:53.889
In terms of cluster
operations and maintenance,

00:34:53.889 --> 00:34:58.700
ephemeral clusters are
meant to be short-running.

00:34:58.700 --> 00:35:00.170
They wouldn't be
highly available.

00:35:00.170 --> 00:35:02.030
You don't need them
to be upgradeable.

00:35:02.030 --> 00:35:05.214
What you're going to do instead
is if you need an upgrade,

00:35:05.214 --> 00:35:06.380
throw away your old cluster.

00:35:06.380 --> 00:35:07.940
Spin up a new one.

00:35:07.940 --> 00:35:12.104
On the other hand, the shared
services cluster is always on.

00:35:12.104 --> 00:35:14.270
All the ephemeral clusters
and long-running clusters

00:35:14.270 --> 00:35:15.780
attach to this.

00:35:15.780 --> 00:35:17.540
So you want this to
be highly available.

00:35:17.540 --> 00:35:18.782
It has to be upgradeable.

00:35:18.782 --> 00:35:20.240
It has to support
running upgrades.

00:35:25.656 --> 00:35:26.780
Putting all this together--

00:35:26.780 --> 00:35:32.570
Cloudbreak 2.7, which is
offered by Hortonworks,

00:35:32.570 --> 00:35:33.950
supports shared
services and lets

00:35:33.950 --> 00:35:36.540
you spin up these environments.

00:35:36.540 --> 00:35:41.630
HDP 2.6.5, as well as 3.0.0,
offers GCS connectivity.

00:35:41.630 --> 00:35:44.810
That's how you can
play with this.

00:35:44.810 --> 00:35:47.146
At this point, I'll
hand it over to Jon

00:35:47.146 --> 00:35:48.795
to talk about Cloudbreak.

00:35:48.795 --> 00:35:49.670
JON DYBIK: Thank you.

00:35:52.841 --> 00:35:53.580
Thanks, Sid.

00:35:57.580 --> 00:35:59.496
So I'm going to talk
about Cloudbreak today.

00:35:59.496 --> 00:36:01.870
And this is really about, how
do we pull everything we've

00:36:01.870 --> 00:36:04.390
talked about today together?

00:36:04.390 --> 00:36:07.360
We've talked about Google
Cloud Storage, shared service

00:36:07.360 --> 00:36:10.260
architecture with ephemeral
workloads in the cloud.

00:36:10.260 --> 00:36:12.905
But how do we actually
get to the cloud?

00:36:12.905 --> 00:36:14.530
How do we get these
workloads and start

00:36:14.530 --> 00:36:18.816
performing big data workloads
on Google Cloud platform?

00:36:18.816 --> 00:36:20.440
And that's really
what Cloudbreak does.

00:36:20.440 --> 00:36:22.590
It's all about
provisioning workloads

00:36:22.590 --> 00:36:24.370
and pulling in a lot
of best practices

00:36:24.370 --> 00:36:26.114
and prescriptive
guidance into the way

00:36:26.114 --> 00:36:28.280
that you provision these
workloads onto Google Cloud

00:36:28.280 --> 00:36:29.560
Platform.

00:36:29.560 --> 00:36:33.580
So if we could actually
switch over to the demo.

00:36:33.580 --> 00:36:34.150
Great.

00:36:34.150 --> 00:36:36.370
The best way to really
talk about Cloudbreak

00:36:36.370 --> 00:36:37.730
is to show it live in action.

00:36:37.730 --> 00:36:39.970
So I'm going to do
a live demo today--

00:36:39.970 --> 00:36:42.160
fingers crossed here--
and kind of walk

00:36:42.160 --> 00:36:47.110
through how you provision
HDP and HDF workloads

00:36:47.110 --> 00:36:49.570
onto Google Cloud Platform.

00:36:49.570 --> 00:36:51.370
So the first thing I
want to point out here

00:36:51.370 --> 00:36:56.740
is I have a data lake shared
services cluster provision,

00:36:56.740 --> 00:36:58.630
just like Sid talked about.

00:36:58.630 --> 00:37:02.080
This is an architecture where
we're externalizing a Hive

00:37:02.080 --> 00:37:03.100
metastore data.

00:37:03.100 --> 00:37:06.760
We're externalizing audit
policies and audit logging

00:37:06.760 --> 00:37:08.830
from the cluster itself
into this shared services

00:37:08.830 --> 00:37:09.554
architecture.

00:37:09.554 --> 00:37:11.470
And this is something
that Cloudbreak support.

00:37:11.470 --> 00:37:15.700
So I've pre-created a data
lake shared services cluster.

00:37:15.700 --> 00:37:17.190
You can see here,
I have a couple

00:37:17.190 --> 00:37:18.580
of external sources set up.

00:37:18.580 --> 00:37:23.590
I have my Hive metastore, as
well as my Ranger database

00:37:23.590 --> 00:37:26.720
and audit logging,
externalized from the clusters

00:37:26.720 --> 00:37:27.762
that I'm going to deploy.

00:37:27.762 --> 00:37:29.053
So that's what I'm going to do.

00:37:29.053 --> 00:37:31.450
I'm going to walk through
creating a new cluster.

00:37:31.450 --> 00:37:34.270
And show you how easy it is to
deploy HDP onto Google Cloud

00:37:34.270 --> 00:37:37.480
Platform as well as attach
that to a shared services

00:37:37.480 --> 00:37:39.280
architecture.

00:37:39.280 --> 00:37:42.870
Quite simply, I'll click
Create Cluster up here.

00:37:42.870 --> 00:37:44.550
And I could choose
the data lake that I

00:37:44.550 --> 00:37:47.370
want to attach it to to get
all those shared services,

00:37:47.370 --> 00:37:49.750
as I spin up these
workloads on demand.

00:37:49.750 --> 00:37:52.625
So I'll choose my
data lake cluster 3.

00:37:52.625 --> 00:37:54.000
And my credentials--
so these are

00:37:54.000 --> 00:37:56.760
credentials that are pre-set
up in Google Cloud Platform.

00:37:56.760 --> 00:38:00.720
This is what allows me to
instantiate instances on GCP

00:38:00.720 --> 00:38:04.537
as well as get HDP
deployed on GCP.

00:38:04.537 --> 00:38:05.620
So let's give this a name.

00:38:05.620 --> 00:38:07.610
We'll call it Demo 3.

00:38:07.610 --> 00:38:09.210
Choose my particular
region here.

00:38:09.210 --> 00:38:13.035
I'm going to stick
with the US East one.

00:38:13.035 --> 00:38:16.050
The platform I
want to deployment,

00:38:16.050 --> 00:38:23.200
whether that's HDP 2.6, whether
that's 3.0, or HDFS as well.

00:38:23.200 --> 00:38:25.140
So let's choose a
data science workload.

00:38:25.140 --> 00:38:29.280
Right out of the box, Cloudbreak
includes several blueprints.

00:38:29.280 --> 00:38:31.560
So if you're familiar
with Ambari blueprints,

00:38:31.560 --> 00:38:33.660
you'll be familiar with
Cloudbreak blueprints.

00:38:33.660 --> 00:38:36.570
And this essentially lets you
define the component services

00:38:36.570 --> 00:38:39.150
and configuration of
those components that

00:38:39.150 --> 00:38:41.544
are going to be deployed
in your HDP cluster.

00:38:41.544 --> 00:38:42.960
So you can see we
have three here.

00:38:42.960 --> 00:38:46.025
One for data science,
an EDW workload, and ETN

00:38:46.025 --> 00:38:48.400
workloads as well, with the
common components that we get

00:38:48.400 --> 00:38:50.290
deployed for those workloads.

00:38:50.290 --> 00:38:52.270
So let's go ahead and
choose data science.

00:38:52.270 --> 00:38:54.440
We'll click Next.

00:38:54.440 --> 00:38:57.440
I'm actually going to
do the advanced UI,

00:38:57.440 --> 00:38:58.750
now that I think about it.

00:38:58.750 --> 00:39:00.110
We have a basic and advanced UI.

00:39:00.110 --> 00:39:02.210
The basic UI is really
good to get you up

00:39:02.210 --> 00:39:03.380
and running very quickly.

00:39:03.380 --> 00:39:06.640
The advanced UI, if you're
going to use the UI,

00:39:06.640 --> 00:39:08.390
is going to be what
most enterprises would

00:39:08.390 --> 00:39:11.450
do to get all the customizations
that they want set up as they

00:39:11.450 --> 00:39:13.230
provision workloads.

00:39:13.230 --> 00:39:16.310
So my image settings here, I'm
going to use the base image

00:39:16.310 --> 00:39:18.170
settings that we ship.

00:39:18.170 --> 00:39:20.390
You can actually create
your own custom images.

00:39:20.390 --> 00:39:22.640
So if you have your own
corporate enterprise standards

00:39:22.640 --> 00:39:26.060
for images for some of the Linux
distributions that we support,

00:39:26.060 --> 00:39:27.560
you can certainly
build those images

00:39:27.560 --> 00:39:31.020
and deploy your corporate
standard images gold standards.

00:39:31.020 --> 00:39:34.130
I'm going to use the
out-of-the-box images we have.

00:39:34.130 --> 00:39:36.140
Hardware and
storage-- so this is

00:39:36.140 --> 00:39:39.510
where I can make some
adjustments to my host groups.

00:39:39.510 --> 00:39:41.970
We have a master node, as
you can see at the far right.

00:39:41.970 --> 00:39:44.420
This is going to be my
Ambari server as well.

00:39:44.420 --> 00:39:47.310
And we have worker nodes and
compute nodes host groups.

00:39:47.310 --> 00:39:49.190
And I can come in
here and adjust

00:39:49.190 --> 00:39:50.880
any of the sizes for them.

00:39:50.880 --> 00:39:52.910
With volumes-- the
number of instances

00:39:52.910 --> 00:39:55.760
that I'm going to deploy
for compute nodes here.

00:39:55.760 --> 00:39:59.840
Let's say we add an instance
here and deploy one instance.

00:40:03.020 --> 00:40:05.390
And we've heard a lot
about cloud storage today.

00:40:05.390 --> 00:40:09.060
That's integrated with
Cloudbreak and, obviously, HDP.

00:40:09.060 --> 00:40:11.090
To do some cloud
storage here, I'm

00:40:11.090 --> 00:40:13.450
going to have to put
in my service account.

00:40:21.930 --> 00:40:24.900
And let's put in my
base storage location.

00:40:29.645 --> 00:40:31.770
And then we could attach
this to the Hive warehouse

00:40:31.770 --> 00:40:33.300
using Google Cloud Storage.

00:40:36.560 --> 00:40:38.780
So cluster extensions,
again, we give you

00:40:38.780 --> 00:40:40.726
very prescriptive
guidance in Cloudbreak

00:40:40.726 --> 00:40:42.600
on how to get these
workloads up and running.

00:40:42.600 --> 00:40:44.683
But we understand, again,
just like custom images,

00:40:44.683 --> 00:40:46.700
there's customizations
that you're probably

00:40:46.700 --> 00:40:47.810
going to need to make.

00:40:47.810 --> 00:40:49.860
So we have this concept
of recipes here.

00:40:49.860 --> 00:40:51.770
This allows you to
run shell scripts.

00:40:51.770 --> 00:40:55.189
And you can determine at
what point during the cluster

00:40:55.189 --> 00:40:57.230
installation that these
scripts are going to run.

00:40:57.230 --> 00:40:59.720
Is it pre-Ambari
being installed?

00:40:59.720 --> 00:41:01.706
Is it post-Ambari
being installed?

00:41:01.706 --> 00:41:03.080
Is it actually a
recipe that runs

00:41:03.080 --> 00:41:05.120
when you terminate the
cluster and remove it,

00:41:05.120 --> 00:41:07.010
as you would ephemeral
workloads when there's

00:41:07.010 --> 00:41:08.540
no longer a need for it?

00:41:08.540 --> 00:41:11.500
But again, these recipes allow
you to run shell scripts.

00:41:11.500 --> 00:41:14.330
And an example of
that would be if you

00:41:14.330 --> 00:41:18.430
needed to join your host to
an active directory domain.

00:41:18.430 --> 00:41:21.080
So you do all the work for that
up front via Ambari install.

00:41:21.080 --> 00:41:22.400
And then when you
terminate the cluster,

00:41:22.400 --> 00:41:23.870
there's probably some
active directory cleanup

00:41:23.870 --> 00:41:25.730
work that you need to
do, and you could have

00:41:25.730 --> 00:41:26.900
a recipe run at that point.

00:41:30.020 --> 00:41:34.600
All right, let's go next and
talk about external sources.

00:41:34.600 --> 00:41:38.580
And this is a new capability
we launched in Cloudbreak 2.7

00:41:38.580 --> 00:41:40.690
is the notion of
dynamic blueprints.

00:41:40.690 --> 00:41:42.420
So very likely,
instead of the need

00:41:42.420 --> 00:41:44.850
to create one-off blueprints
for several different

00:41:44.850 --> 00:41:47.580
configurations
that you need, you

00:41:47.580 --> 00:41:50.250
could have a single
blueprint and inject settings

00:41:50.250 --> 00:41:51.120
into that blueprint.

00:41:51.120 --> 00:41:53.517
So you can do things like
one-time configurations.

00:41:53.517 --> 00:41:55.350
Common would be if you
want to connect these

00:41:55.350 --> 00:41:58.530
to an l-dot directory and do
things, like single sign on.

00:41:58.530 --> 00:42:03.120
I have an l-dot directory set
up here, an active directory.

00:42:03.120 --> 00:42:06.587
You can configure this to
connect to external databases.

00:42:06.587 --> 00:42:08.670
These are probably one-time
configuration settings

00:42:08.670 --> 00:42:13.530
that you want to set up and just
be able to easily select those.

00:42:13.530 --> 00:42:14.370
Next.

00:42:14.370 --> 00:42:19.380
Now during configuration, Sid
talked a little bit about Knox.

00:42:19.380 --> 00:42:21.690
We have that ability to
provision with Cloudbreak

00:42:21.690 --> 00:42:25.890
and wrap your clusters
with an Apache Knox powered

00:42:25.890 --> 00:42:27.180
secured gateway.

00:42:27.180 --> 00:42:30.840
This is great for limiting
surface area of ports that

00:42:30.840 --> 00:42:32.890
are exposed to the
public internet

00:42:32.890 --> 00:42:35.160
and really securing the
services that you want

00:42:35.160 --> 00:42:39.360
to publish for your cluster.

00:42:39.360 --> 00:42:40.620
So we can do that right here.

00:42:40.620 --> 00:42:42.690
Very prescriptive
guidance, we can wrap that.

00:42:42.690 --> 00:42:45.000
Then we get to the
network configuration.

00:42:45.000 --> 00:42:47.850
We can select existing
networks and subnets

00:42:47.850 --> 00:42:48.852
we want to attach to.

00:42:48.852 --> 00:42:50.310
We need to make
sure, in this case,

00:42:50.310 --> 00:42:52.980
because we're attaching to a
data lake services cluster,

00:42:52.980 --> 00:42:56.790
that whatever selected network
and subnet you have defined

00:42:56.790 --> 00:43:01.237
can communicate with the shared
services cluster as well.

00:43:01.237 --> 00:43:03.320
You can come in here and
edit the security groups.

00:43:03.320 --> 00:43:05.190
You can create a
new security group,

00:43:05.190 --> 00:43:07.620
edit your existing
security groups.

00:43:07.620 --> 00:43:11.140
We intelligently know what
services you want to deploy.

00:43:11.140 --> 00:43:12.810
So we're exposing
the correct ports

00:43:12.810 --> 00:43:15.270
needed for that,
like 9.4.4.3.22,

00:43:15.270 --> 00:43:17.220
and 9.4.4.3 for
Cloudbreak communication

00:43:17.220 --> 00:43:23.090
with the cluster, and 8.4.4.3,
and port 22 for SSH as well.

00:43:27.660 --> 00:43:29.940
And some last security
configurations

00:43:29.940 --> 00:43:30.760
that I need to do.

00:43:30.760 --> 00:43:40.440
Give my Ambari admin a
password and put in my SSH key,

00:43:40.440 --> 00:43:45.010
so I can show into the boxes.

00:43:48.639 --> 00:43:50.960
We did support Kerberos as well.

00:43:50.960 --> 00:43:52.190
You can enable Kerberos.

00:43:52.190 --> 00:43:55.670
If you want to do that, there
are some prerequisite checks

00:43:55.670 --> 00:43:58.130
that you need to go through
to make sure that's set up

00:43:58.130 --> 00:43:59.750
for your environment correctly.

00:43:59.750 --> 00:44:01.140
If you're just
doing some testing

00:44:01.140 --> 00:44:02.931
and getting up with
Cloudbreak really quick

00:44:02.931 --> 00:44:05.300
to see how easy it is to
spin up workloads or try

00:44:05.300 --> 00:44:08.640
some workloads on your own,
you can use a test KDC.

00:44:08.640 --> 00:44:09.950
We use an MIT KDC.

00:44:09.950 --> 00:44:12.710
But again, this is only
suitable for a test environment,

00:44:12.710 --> 00:44:13.970
not suitable for production.

00:44:13.970 --> 00:44:17.147
You should bring your own KDC.

00:44:17.147 --> 00:44:18.730
And then, very simply,
create cluster.

00:44:23.620 --> 00:44:26.600
You can see my new cluster
being created here.

00:44:26.600 --> 00:44:29.360
Depending upon how many nodes
you're deploying and whatnot,

00:44:29.360 --> 00:44:31.460
it usually takes
about 20 minutes here

00:44:31.460 --> 00:44:34.280
to get through a complete
cluster deployment, instantiate

00:44:34.280 --> 00:44:38.300
all the VM Instances as well
as do the Ambari install,

00:44:38.300 --> 00:44:40.475
and then add all the nodes
to the cluster as well.

00:44:40.475 --> 00:44:42.170
When it's done, your
cluster is going

00:44:42.170 --> 00:44:44.378
to look similar to this one
that I've already created

00:44:44.378 --> 00:44:46.520
and attached to my data lake.

00:44:46.520 --> 00:44:49.430
You can see here, here's
the gateway configuration

00:44:49.430 --> 00:44:51.380
with all the endpoints
for all the services

00:44:51.380 --> 00:44:53.330
that I published
through Apache Knox.

00:44:53.330 --> 00:44:57.700
I can see all my external
sources that are attached

00:44:57.700 --> 00:45:00.560
as well as the cloud
storage that I have attached

00:45:00.560 --> 00:45:02.630
to this particular cluster.

00:45:02.630 --> 00:45:05.540
One popular feature for our
customers is auto scaling.

00:45:05.540 --> 00:45:06.890
So once they get on the cloud--

00:45:06.890 --> 00:45:09.080
and I'm working with
ephemeral workloads--

00:45:09.080 --> 00:45:12.200
I can actually
resize this workload

00:45:12.200 --> 00:45:14.240
and add additional
compute nodes.

00:45:14.240 --> 00:45:17.600
I can add additional
worker nodes manually.

00:45:17.600 --> 00:45:20.245
But auto scaling is a
great advanced feature here

00:45:20.245 --> 00:45:24.380
that we have that allows you
to scale up and down based

00:45:24.380 --> 00:45:26.300
on different events
that happened.

00:45:26.300 --> 00:45:29.750
So for example here,
we have alerts.

00:45:29.750 --> 00:45:32.300
And that's the basic
from which you're

00:45:32.300 --> 00:45:34.780
going to create policies that
are going to happen here.

00:45:34.780 --> 00:45:36.110
And alerts can be time-based.

00:45:36.110 --> 00:45:37.485
They're based on
Cron Expression.

00:45:37.485 --> 00:45:39.540
So at particular
times during the day,

00:45:39.540 --> 00:45:42.724
if you want to grow the workload
that you have to provision

00:45:42.724 --> 00:45:44.390
on the Cloud, or you
need to shrink it--

00:45:44.390 --> 00:45:47.430
let's say it's a batch job or
something that runs at night,

00:45:47.430 --> 00:45:49.040
and you need to
scale up for that--

00:45:49.040 --> 00:45:51.030
you could certainly
do that based on time.

00:45:51.030 --> 00:45:55.680
You can also do that based on
any Ambari metrics as well.

00:45:55.680 --> 00:45:57.200
So based on what
you see going on

00:45:57.200 --> 00:46:01.730
in that cluster, if you need
to grow or contract and whatnot

00:46:01.730 --> 00:46:04.160
and take advantage of
elasticity in the cloud,

00:46:04.160 --> 00:46:06.800
you can certainly do that.

00:46:06.800 --> 00:46:10.090
And then from the auto
scaling policy's perspective,

00:46:10.090 --> 00:46:11.200
again, you can add nodes.

00:46:11.200 --> 00:46:12.200
You can remove nodes.

00:46:12.200 --> 00:46:14.420
You can also set
the number of nodes

00:46:14.420 --> 00:46:17.230
to a particular number
of nodes that you want.

00:46:17.230 --> 00:46:18.730
And then you define
which host group

00:46:18.730 --> 00:46:23.102
that this policy applies to.

00:46:23.102 --> 00:46:23.810
All right, great.

00:46:23.810 --> 00:46:25.997
So that's it for
the demonstration.

00:46:25.997 --> 00:46:27.830
Just one thing I want
to mention real quick,

00:46:27.830 --> 00:46:29.270
and then we'll go to questions.

00:46:29.270 --> 00:46:31.290
We got some time for questions.

00:46:31.290 --> 00:46:32.300
We do have a booth.

00:46:32.300 --> 00:46:34.269
It's on the third floor.

00:46:34.269 --> 00:46:35.810
If you're coming
from the escalators,

00:46:35.810 --> 00:46:37.460
it's all the way straight back.

00:46:37.460 --> 00:46:38.664
It's facing the stairs.

00:46:38.664 --> 00:46:40.080
So come check us
out at the booth.

00:46:40.080 --> 00:46:43.430
We can demos for you, talk
about any of the products

00:46:43.430 --> 00:46:45.920
that we have, and then answer
any questions that you have.

00:46:45.920 --> 00:46:47.336
So thank you very
much for coming.

00:46:47.336 --> 00:46:49.960
[MUSIC PLAYING]

